{UTF-8}
Perceived Marginal Cost=A FUNCTION OF(Perceived Marginal Cost,Marginal Cost,Time to Perceive Marginal Cost\
		) ~~|
Perceived Marginal Cost=
	~	$/mmbtu
	~	Developers' belief about the marginal costs of new wells.
	|

Reference Capacity Under Development= INITIAL(
	Capacity Under Development)
	~	Thousand Cubic Feet/Year
	~	To ensure that the model starts in equilibrium, the reference level of \
		capacity under development is set to the initial value of the stock of \
		capacity under development.
	|

Production Capacity= INTEG (
	Project Completion-Decommissioning,
		Reference Production Capacity)
	~	Thousand Cubic Feet/Year
	~	Total natural gas production capacity, measured in million BTU/year of \
		potential production, determined by the number of producing wells and \
		average well productivity.
	|

Marginal Cost=
	Reference Marginal Cost*Long Run Supply Curve(Total Exploited Resource/Reference Exploited Resource\
		)*Effect of Fracking on Marginal Costs
	~	$/tcf
	~	The marginal cost of new wells is determined by the long run supply curve, \
		which in turn is a function of the total exploited resource relative to a \
		reference level.  As total production capacity (including capacity under \
		development) increases relative to the reference level, the marginal cost \
		of the next project increases.  To simulate the impact of technological \
		improvement, marginal costs shift by a fraction given by the Supply Curve \
		Shift at a specified Supply Curve Shift Time.
	|

Demand=
	Reference Production Capacity
	~	Thousand Cubic Feet/Year
	~	For this simple model we set demand to a constant, the initial equilibrium \
		value given by reference production capacity.  A more sophisticated model \
		would represent demand as depening on stocks of natural gas consuming \
		plant and equipment (buildings, factories, power plants, etc.) and their \
		capacity utilization.
	|

Effect of Fracking on Marginal Costs=
	1+STEP(Supply Curve Shift,Supply Curve Shift Time)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Fracking shifts the supply curve by a specified amount at a specified time.
	|

Reference Exploited Resource=
	Reference Production Capacity+Reference Capacity Under Development
	~	Thousand Cubic Feet/Year
	~	The long run supply curve is normalized by the reference total exploited \
		resource, consisting of the reference (initial) capacity plus capacity \
		under development, that is, the total capacity entailed by all exploited \
		sites.
	|

Supply Curve Shift=
	-0.2
	~	Dmnl
	~	To simulate the impact of technological change, the marginal cost of new \
		capacity shifts by this fraction at a specified time.  A value of -.2, for \
		example, reduces marginal costs by 20%.
	|

Expected Price=A FUNCTION OF(Expected Price,Price,Time to Form Price Expectations) ~~|
Expected Price=
	~	$/tcf
	~	The price investors and developers expect at the time investments \
		initiated now are completed
	|

Reference Marginal Cost=
	6
	~	$/tcf
	~	The reference (initial) marginal cost of production, in $/tcf.
	|

Long Run Supply Curve(
	[(0,0)-(4,60)],(0,0.4),(0.5,0.6),(1,1),(1.5,1.6),(2,2.5),(2.5,5),(3,10),(4,50))
	~	Dmnl
	~	The long run supply curve determines how marginal costs rise as the total \
		exploited resource increases relative to the reference level.
	|

Reference Production Capacity=
	2e+010
	~	Thousand Cubic Feet/Year
	~	To ensure that the model begins in equilibrium, reference production \
		capacity is set to a constant that approximates actual production for the \
		period before the advent of fracking.  Total consumption in the US in the \
		2000s averaged approximately 20-22 billion btu/year (based on consumption \
		of about 20 million cubic feet/year and an average of 1020 BTU/cubic foot).
	|

Supply Curve Shift Time=
	0
	~	Year
	~	The year in which the exogenous technological shift in marginal costs \
		occurs.
	|

Price=
	Perceived Marginal Cost*(Demand/Production Capacity)^(1/Sensitivity of Utilization to Profit\
		)
	~	$/tcf
	~	The spot price is modeled as a reference level modified by the \
		demand/supply balance.  To clear the market, price must take the level \
		that causes utilization of production capacity to rise or fall just enough \
		to satisfy the market clearing condition that Demand = Production Capacity \
		* Capacity Utilization.  Capacity utilization, U, can be modeled simply as \
		U = f(P/P*) = (P/P*)^s where P is price, P* is the marginal cost perceived \
		by market participants, and s is the sensitivity of utilization to price.  \
		Solving for price yields the equation here.
	|

Decommissioning=A FUNCTION OF(Decommissioning,Average Life of Capacity,Project Completion\
		) ~~|
Decommissioning=
	~	Thousand Cubic Feet/Year/Year
	~	The rate at which existing production infrastructure is decommissioned.
	|

Project Completion=A FUNCTION OF(Project Completion,Average Development Delay,Project Initiation\
		) ~~|
Project Completion=
	~	Thousand Cubic Feet/Year/Year
	~	The rate at which development projects are completed and add to production \
		capacity.
	|

Project Initiation=
	MAX(0, Decommissioning + Effect of Expected Profit on Investment*Production Capacity\
		)
	~	Thousand Cubic Feet/Year/Year
	~	The number of new wells being initiated each year, measured in terms of \
		production capacity (tcf/year) started per year.  Projects are initiated \
		to replace capacity that is being decommissioned, adjusted by the effect \
		of expected profitability on project starts.  When expected profits are \
		high, developers initiate more projects and vice versa.  The number of new \
		projects started is proportional to existing capacity.  The Initiation \
		rate is constrained to be nonnegative:  even if development were extremely \
		unprofitable, the initiation rate cannot fall below zero.
	|

Sensitivity of Utilization to Profit=
	0.2
	~	Dmnl
	~	The sensitivity of utilization to profit determines the response of \
		capacity utilization as it depends on price.
	|

Total Exploited Resource=
	Production Capacity+Capacity Under Development
	~	Thousand Cubic Feet/Year
	~	The total exploited resource is the sum of current capacity and capacity \
		under development.
	|

Capacity Under Development= INTEG (
	Project Initiation-Project Completion,
		Average Development Delay*Decommissioning)
	~	Thousand Cubic Feet/Year
	~	Total capacity under development, including projects being planned.
	|

Average Development Delay=A FUNCTION OF(Average Development Delay) ~~|
Average Development Delay=
	~	Year
	~	The average time between project initiatiion and completion.
	|

Effect of Expected Profit on Investment=
	Sensitivity of Investment to Profitability*Expected Profit
	~	1/Year
	~	Development increases when expected profits are high and contracts when \
		expected profits are low.  The sensitivity parameter is the fractional \
		rate of expansion or contraction per year as a fraction of existing \
		production capacity.
	|

Expected Profit=
	(Expected Price-Perceived Marginal Cost)/Perceived Marginal Cost
	~	Dmnl
	~	Expected profit, as a fraction of perceived marginal costs of development.  \
		The expected profit margin is the difference between expected price and \
		perceived marginal cost.  Marginal costs here include the required return \
		on capital, so in equilibrium expected profits are zero.  That is, \
		expected profit represents economic, not accounting profitability.
	|

Sensitivity of Investment to Profitability=
	0.1
	~	1/Year
	~	The sensitivity of investment to profitability is the fractional rate per \
		year of capacity increase or decrease as a function of expected \
		profitability.  The larger the value the more aggressively developers \
		respond to abnormal profits.
	|

Time to Form Price Expectations=A FUNCTION OF(Time to Form Price Expectations) ~~|
Time to Form Price Expectations=
	~	Year
	~	The average time required for developers to update their beliefs about the \
		future price.
	|

Time to Perceive Marginal Cost=A FUNCTION OF(Time to Perceive Marginal Cost) ~~|
Time to Perceive Marginal Cost=
	~	Year
	~	The average time required for developers to perceive changes in true \
		marginal costs
	|

Average Life of Capacity=A FUNCTION OF(Average Life of Capacity) ~~|
Average Life of Capacity=
	~	Year
	~	The average useful life of wells and associated production equipment.
	|

********************************************************
	.Control
********************************************************~
		Simulation Control Parameters
	|

FINAL TIME  = 40
	~	Year
	~	The final time for the simulation.
	|

INITIAL TIME  = -2
	~	Year
	~	The initial time for the simulation.
	|

SAVEPER  = 
        TIME STEP
	~	Year [0,?]
	~	The frequency with which output is stored.
	|

TIME STEP  = 0.015625
	~	Year [0,?]
	~	The time step for the simulation.
	|

